rrors from Huawei Cloud and Tsinghua University. GPG key signing and secure package verification ensure system integrity, while tools like createrepo and Reproducible Builds (adopted since 2022) enhance trust and transparency. The project includes advanced frameworks such as secGear for confidential computing, the Gazelle networking stack, and initiatives like Euler-Copilot, A-Ops, and gala-ops to support system automation and deployment.\n\nSecurity is prioritized through SELinux policies, PAM_wheel.so enforcement, a dedicated security committee, and the publication of secure configuration benchmarks. openEuler supports a wide array of applications, including Apache, MySQL, Zabbix, ROS2 Humble, NVIDIA graphics drivers, openGauss, QGIS, and distributed soft bus systems, while also supporting kernel development, driver compilation, AI inference, and big data processing. It is compatible with popular software stacks and deployment environments, including Kubernetes via Eggops and containerized workloads via iSulad.\n\nDespite some technical limitations—such as missing default Docker packages in certain versions, lack of Python 2 support from 22.03 LTS onward, restricted partitioning during installation, and incompatibility with software like Oracle, ZeroTier, Mellanox OFED, and Cloudera Manager Agent—openEuler continues to expand its ecosystem. Challenges related to SSH, GRUB, and network connectivity have been reported but are actively addressed. The project thrives through community collaboration via Special Interest Groups (SIGs), public forums, documentation, and transparent governance. It plays a central role in China’s national innovation (xinchuang) initiative and has gained international attention through global outreach, conferences, and recognition on platforms like DistroWatch, underscoring its impact as a unified, open, and future-ready operating system.<SEP>openEuler is a Linux-based open-source operating system designed for enterprise use.<SEP>openEuler 